WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:33.113 --> 00:00:38.942
– What would you say to him is one proof
that he can look at to confirm that

00:00:38.953 --> 00:00:41.848
the Quran is not a man-made book

00:00:41.859 --> 00:00:44.524
and Islam is not a man-made religion,
it is indeed from the divine,

00:00:44.535 --> 00:00:46.439
being an expert in the Quran?

00:00:47.013 --> 00:00:52.445
– Well, lots can be said,
I'd start off by my own journey to share.

00:00:52.456 --> 00:00:54.321
– Can you hear him Andrew?

00:00:54.332 --> 00:00:55.823
– Yeah.

00:00:56.313 --> 00:00:57.617
– Nice talking to you.

00:00:57.628 --> 00:01:02.093
I think that what I like to do
when I meet with audiences,

00:01:02.093 --> 00:01:06.161
professors of other faiths
and intellectuals, anybody

00:01:06.172 --> 00:01:13.355
is that I like to invite them to explore
the Quran intellectually and critically.

00:01:13.366 --> 00:01:16.166
that's an invitation that the Quran gives.

00:01:16.177 --> 00:01:21.247
It's interesting that people perceive religion 
as something that you have to be 
indoctrinated into

00:01:21.258 --> 00:01:24.158
and you have to follow it as the dictates are

00:01:24.169 --> 00:01:28.054
and you don't have to actually 
critically ask any questions 
or think and contemplate.

00:01:28.065 --> 00:01:32.425
And the Quran is kind of unique in the way
that it presents its message because

00:01:32.436 --> 00:01:34.807
it's constantly saying
"Don't you then think?

00:01:34.818 --> 00:01:37.363
Don't you contemplate?
Haven't you asked questions?"

00:01:37.374 --> 00:01:41.047
This is a book for those
who seek to have answers.

00:01:41.058 --> 00:01:45.024
It's not actually asking you to shut 
your brain off and accept what it's saying,

00:01:45.035 --> 00:01:47.827
it's actually asking you 
to contemplate its message

00:01:47.838 --> 00:01:50.803
so it stands really unique in that sense.

00:01:53.307 --> 00:01:55.241
Since it offered that invitation to me,

00:01:55.252 --> 00:01:58.931
I didn't come to the Quran first 
as someone who believes in it,

00:01:58.942 --> 00:02:02.321
I wanted to figure this thing out
and you know trying to make sense of it.

00:02:02.332 --> 00:02:06.577
And many of the things that
I struggled with in philosophy

00:02:06.588 --> 00:02:08.921
when I was a student of philosophy,

00:02:08.932 --> 00:02:13.439
they started getting unraveled as I was getting 
deeper and deeper in my study of the Quran

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:17.416
it just started kind of untying
a lot of those knots

00:02:17.427 --> 00:02:20.974
and it's also remarkable that
some of the biggest addictions

00:02:20.985 --> 00:02:27.215
that people suffer from, every one of them
is targeting one after the other in the Quran.

00:02:27.226 --> 00:02:30.276
Some of the things that
like gambling for example,

00:02:30.287 --> 00:02:33.372
alcohol for example,
intoxication for example,

00:02:33.383 --> 00:02:39.098
like each of these things is targeting 
and you think it's not just solving 
a Muslim problem,

00:02:39.109 --> 00:02:44.297
it's solving a human problem, 
it's solving a societal problem 
and solving a global problem

00:02:44.308 --> 00:02:47.520
by targeting each of these specific things.

00:02:47.531 --> 00:02:52.891
The other thing that really fascinated me
I ended up writing a book on it,

00:02:52.891 --> 00:02:55.401
ended up getting taught
around the world AlhamduliLlah,

00:02:55.412 --> 00:02:59.585
it's even being used 
in one of the Islamic studies courses 
at Harvard now.

00:02:59.596 --> 00:03:00.807
It's "Divine speech",

00:03:00.818 --> 00:03:04.450
it's a book I dedicated along
with my student to why is this book,

00:03:04.461 --> 00:03:07.733
why am I believing that it's divine,

00:03:07.744 --> 00:03:08.795
what's making me think this?

00:03:08.806 --> 00:03:11.263
and I wrote it for 
a non-muslim audience actually.

00:03:11.274 --> 00:03:15.715
It's a little bit academic but the point of it
was there are there are elements to this book

00:03:15.726 --> 00:03:19.185
and the way that it's structured 
that if you went to the library

00:03:19.196 --> 00:03:21.981
or you got on Amazon and bought yourself 
a translation of the Quran,

00:03:21.992 --> 00:03:23.289
you started reading it,

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:26.332
you think the subject is kind of 
going all over the place,

00:03:26.343 --> 00:03:30.750
it's not staying on the same subject,
it's saying some profound things

00:03:30.761 --> 00:03:34.146
but the organization seems
unlike anything I've ever read.

00:03:34.157 --> 00:03:36.542
and that was one of the things
that baffled me at first like,

00:03:36.553 --> 00:03:38.497
"Why is it organized in this way?"

00:03:38.508 --> 00:03:42.395
And so one of the areas of my study
became the organization of the Quran,

00:03:42.406 --> 00:03:44.063
why is it organized in this way?

00:03:44.074 --> 00:03:47.262
Why is God talking in this disarray?

00:03:47.273 --> 00:03:50.396
And what I discovered was
something absolutely breathtaking,

00:03:50.407 --> 00:03:55.808
that it's it's got a symmetrical structure,
that you'll have a chapter for example,

00:03:55.819 --> 00:03:59.935
that's hundreds of verses
like Bakara is 286 verses,

00:03:59.946 --> 00:04:02.141
it's an oral tradition 
so it wasn't written first,

00:04:02.152 --> 00:04:05.946
it was recited and pronounced
and memorized in that way,

00:04:05.957 --> 00:04:08.267
amounts to about 50 pages in Arabic.

00:04:08.278 --> 00:04:11.273
But if you study the subject matter,

00:04:11.284 --> 00:04:18.716
the nine subjects that are occurring
in this long 50 page discourse

00:04:18.727 --> 00:04:22.055
are like subject one 
is directly tied to subject nine

00:04:22.066 --> 00:04:24.400
and two to eight and three to seven

00:04:24.411 --> 00:04:27.828
and four to six and five
right center in the middle,

00:04:27.839 --> 00:04:30.830
like there's an incredible
three-dimensional structure

00:04:30.841 --> 00:04:32.665
to the way the arguments being presented.

00:04:32.676 --> 00:04:35.081
Human beings we think linear,
"I'm going to make point a,

00:04:35.092 --> 00:04:37.497
then I'm going to make point b,
then I'm going to make point c,

00:04:37.508 --> 00:04:39.618
that's how I organize my thoughts",

00:04:39.629 --> 00:04:41.914
and unless you write something down

00:04:41.925 --> 00:04:47.474
and say you know "What I'm going to do this 
and just for fun I'm going to do abc 
and then go cba".

00:04:47.485 --> 00:04:50.429
Even doing that in six sentences is hard for me

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:53.579
as I'm sitting here talking to you
because my brain isn't wired that way,

00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:57.432
but this a millennium and a half old tradition

00:04:57.443 --> 00:05:01.229
has got multiple Surahs
and multiple chapters,

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:03.532
where this kind of a structure 
is demonstrated over

00:05:03.543 --> 00:05:05.791
and over again and other kinds of structures.

00:05:05.802 --> 00:05:11.357
This is not possible for a human being 
to do linguistically, it's not possible, 
I'm a student of linguistics,

00:05:11.368 --> 00:05:15.628
it's just not possible these kinds 
of structures, this kind of organization,

00:05:15.639 --> 00:05:17.643
that that's one of the things
I wrote about in the book

00:05:17.654 --> 00:05:20.793
and I started ended up
teaching courses on this stuff.

00:05:20.804 --> 00:05:24.706
But for just just take a step back level,

00:05:24.717 --> 00:05:29.097
my invitation to anybody 
is put your preconceived notions aside,

00:05:29.108 --> 00:05:31.139
take what you may have heard about Islam,

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:34.233
what you may have thought about it
from your own faith traditions point of view,

00:05:34.244 --> 00:05:38.684
put all of that aside and I wouldn't even invite
somebody to read the Quran to accept Islam.

00:05:38.695 --> 00:05:45.410
Read the Quran neutrally 
and get a first an unbiased impression

00:05:45.421 --> 00:05:48.314
and I would think it's really
difficult to not walk away

00:05:48.325 --> 00:05:52.405
truly being moved by what
you're what you're being exposed to,

00:05:52.416 --> 00:05:54.874
truly being hit by what you're exposed to.

00:05:54.885 --> 00:05:59.465
The final thing I'll say is there is
a huge tragedy in the world today

00:05:59.476 --> 00:06:04.176
that even most Muslims aren't as aware 
of the Quran as they should be.

00:06:04.187 --> 00:06:07.580
So Muslims don't become
a really good representation

00:06:07.591 --> 00:06:08.868
of the contents of the book.

00:06:08.879 --> 00:06:12.143
So even disconnecting yourself 
from the Muslim bias,

00:06:12.154 --> 00:06:14.700
"I'm just going to read this for myself,

00:06:14.711 --> 00:06:17.497
with no other influences as much as possible".

00:06:17.508 --> 00:06:21.294
At the end of the day human beings
are going to have some bias or the other,

00:06:21.305 --> 00:06:26.608
but as much as I can consciously be disconnected
from bias and give it a shot and read it

00:06:26.619 --> 00:06:29.175
I think that would be my invitation to anybody.

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:34.313
– Andrew, any question, any final question
you'd like to ask him while he's here?

00:06:34.324 --> 00:06:37.396
What happened was because
we got delayed with the program,

00:06:37.407 --> 00:06:41.200
next guest was coming in, so I'm with you.

00:06:41.211 --> 00:06:44.636
But this is amazing, 
you can look him up actually online

00:06:44.647 --> 00:06:46.980
and you'll see a ton of his videos.

00:06:46.991 --> 00:06:48.848
– I'm going to, I'm going to read 
that book as well,

00:06:48.859 --> 00:06:50.849
because it was really interesting 
what you were saying.

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:53.599
– After the show I'd like to send you 
a copy as a gift.

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:56.369
– And also your invitation is exactly
the way I would have done it anyway,

00:06:56.380 --> 00:06:59.859
I would have read it, I'm going to read it,
I've yet to read the Quran,

00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:03.236
but it's certainly something I'm going to do, 
I'm going to read it without 
a preconceived notion,

00:07:03.247 --> 00:07:06.519
without a preconceived idea 
and I think that's the best way to do things

00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:08.688
because you're right, 
there's too much bias in the world.

00:07:08.699 --> 00:07:12.331
From what I know, 
I think we just talked about for the last hour,

00:07:12.342 --> 00:07:15.007
from what I know,
I have absolute respect for the religion

00:07:15.018 --> 00:07:18.380
and respect for the faith
and I've been very blessed to be on the show,

00:07:18.391 --> 00:07:20.139
so thank you very much for having me, 
I appreciate it.

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:20.420
